{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the design note on how a register retires a founder, at head 4a8cd752, for request 788d0ec8 and review request 2c5f0f76. Verdict: APPROVED, as a design with no code built. The operator's view of the log no longer depends on the thief, the lifetime limit is stated and raised, and a stopped claim gets a final answer. The operator key's read stays the planner's to rule on, as the note says, and the planner should weigh one point below. The note may land.\n\nAll of this comes from reading the code, as the note does. Nothing ran.\n\nYou corrected me twice, and you are right both times. I said that a final `retired-founder` might not fit \"because the list reads it\", and your reason is the better one: the summary omits final items, so a client could not compute the list. And I assumed that start custody could hold such a claim, and on this cohort it cannot.\n\n## Your three questions\n\n1. **The operator key's read with no window: nothing it should not read, and one claim in the note is too strong.**\n   - **What it reads.** The register's summary, history and entries. These hold each `found` intent's fields, and each claim's seed and repository. Nothing in the register holds a room's content.\n   - **What else reads the root actor.** `checkedRead` uses `chains.at(0)` through `leads`, and `#rooted` already finds that root for a key with no recent entry, as you cite. I found no other user of it at a register. The census makes its own check against the genesis, so this changes nothing there. The set of pins keeps today's one-key registers out, which is the right limit.\n   - **The strong claim.** \"The rule gives it no new knowledge\" holds for what the key can learn. It does not hold for who can see that it looked. Today the key must sign `add-founder` to read the log, and that leaves an entry and spends one of 256 additions. Under the new rule, a thief with a stolen operator key reads the log with no trace, for as long as the register lasts, and learns of each new room as it is claimed. Your cost line says that the key \"reads the log until then\", and it does not say that this read is silent. This is the point I would put to the planner. I do not ask you to change the design for it. Add that one sentence when you next touch the note, or the planner's ruling can state it.\n2. **Client paths on a final claim: the paths you name stop, and two others remain.**\n   - **A first run already in its wait.** The settlement is read at the start of a rerun. A `dap create` that is already reading the directory when the retirement lands gives up after 120 reads, as it does today. The rerun then stops. So \"each path stops at once\" holds for a rerun, and that is acceptable. Word it that way.\n   - **`dap mcp`.** A person who signs `found` through the `act` tool settles with the `settle` tool, and then a model may `wait` on the directory. The note's list leaves out this surface. The new member reaches it only if `settle` passes it on. Add `settle` to the build list.\n   - I found no client that loops on a final state in code that I read. I did not read the whole of `claim.ts`.\n3. **Can a thief's self-retirement make a command print something false: no.** After a self-retirement, `founder-retired` is true when Rita's act arrives. \"The register already refuses every found by this key\" and \"Nothing changed\" are both true of that act. The lines point to the log, which now holds who retired it. Reaching those words needs the grant first, so only the operator or that key gets them. `not-a-founder` reports a key that was never listed, and a thief cannot cause that. The `stopped` lines say \"the retirement of key…\", which stays true whoever signed it.\n\n## The rest of the repair\n\n- **Item 1.** The sixth rule, and its rejected answers, cover the scene. `checkedRead` is the right place, because a definition states no read rule.\n- **Item 2.** `max: 256` and `max: 288` add correctly, and `new-founder` keeps the current list at 32. A larger `max` also grows the summary, which holds every live item up to `type.max`, and you state that cost. On a very old register that is about 600 items, so the build should confirm that a summary of that size still reads as complete on the clients.\n- **The table's added row.** A claim with `repository` set whose `create` is refused stays `pending`, as you now state. That is a place in `max` that nothing frees today, separate from this design. The note's trace of that path stops at child admission and says so.\n- **The earlier nits.** The rejected mutable list, `derivedEffects`, `most: 64`, the install guard under every policy, and the line about a Git read token are all in.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n**Architecture.** Sound. The journal stays the record, and the existing hooks do the stopping. Reading the settled item's state on the settlement reply is a small addition, and the exact signed intent already authorizes that reply.\n\n**Security.** The split of signers holds, and a thief's first move no longer costs the operator the log. The cost moves to a stolen operator key, which gets a silent read. That is the trade the planner rules on.\n\n**Simplification.** Acceptable. The design adds two item types, one read rule and one reply member, and each replaces something that would otherwise need a new act.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- **Read.** The diff from 764a7866, `checkLocalSignedRead`, `checkedRead`, `#rooted`, the summary's reading in `reads.ts`, and the MCP tools' list. I did not read `core.ts`'s settlement or the page's `claim.ts` lines that the note cites.\n- **Checks at this head.** The head adds one file against main ad411431. The worktree stayed clean. I ran no test, because no code changes.\n- **Not done.** I used no independent reader. Nothing here has run, and the note says so.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:655c513ddcd35a8d85b5a200b7a9a85515f70d84","binding":"assigned","head":"4a8cd7520c5d79be3d347615c436c56e1bf548b1","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:eea1b172998c09ad6b7ac2c90b674280944d4f0b\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:dbeccea39db0dedbb950bfde0e1dd02692e0ff6a\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e0e713078ad2f81297f8e576141ee5e36f0cd1bd","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}